  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full paper text is present, including the appendix and checklist.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper uses the distinction between memorization and overfitting to motivate a membership inference attack. It reports train/test perplexity, but does not test the claim versions about random-label fitting, width, image architectures, optimizers, batch size, dropout, sharpness, or removing memorized examples.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports target-model train/test perplexity and states that none of the fine-tuned LLMs exhibit significant overfitting. These results concern the claim’s generalization topic but do not measure a version’s named behavior. Attack AUC and privacy-budget results are not rows because they evaluate the paper’s membership inference method, not the claim’s generalization behavior. Cited prior-work results are excluded.",
    "test_reason": "The study uses memorization and generalization to motivate membership inference, including citing V8's original source. Its own results measure attack performance and language-model perplexity, without testing any version's specified comparison or intervention. Neither membership detection nor similar training/test perplexity tests random-label fitting or the effect of removing memorized examples.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate: the authors attribute existing attacks' practical weaknesses to reference-data requirements and dependence on overfitting, and conclude that SPV-MIA improves membership inference using self-prompt calibration and a memorization-based signal. Appendix A.5.4 characterizes the target models as showing no significant overfitting; this is the authors' description, without reported significance tests.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Fine-tuning of causal language models with AdamW, early stopping, and mostly LoRA; the paper evaluates membership inference rather than the claim’s generalization interventions.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported train/test perplexity differs by dataset and model; no uncertainty estimates are reported.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Table 9 reports training/test perplexities for four models on three datasets. Test perplexity exceeds training perplexity in all twelve pairs; the authors characterize these differences as no significant overfitting.",
        "proposed_claim": "Under this fine-tuning setup, causal language models have relatively similar training and test perplexities.",
